Looking at this problem, I need to prove that `Total(s + t) == Total(s) + Total(t)` for both `AppendA` and `AppendB` lemmas.

The `Total` function is defined recursively on the first element:
- Base case: `|s| == 0` returns 0
- Recursive case: `s[0] + Total(s[1..])`

For the lemma, I need to do induction on `s`:
- Base case: When `|s| == 0`, we have `s + t == t`, so `Total(s + t) == Total(t) == 0 + Total(t) == Total(s) + Total(t)`
- Inductive case: When `|s| > 0`, we need to show `Total(s + t) == Total(s) + Total(t)` using the recursive call on `s[1..]`

Key insight: When `|s| > 0`:
- `(s + t)[0] == s[0]`
- `(s + t)[1..] == s[1..] + t`

So `Total(s + t) == s[0] + Total((s + t)[1..]) == s[0] + Total(s[1..] + t)`

By induction hypothesis: `Total(s[1..] + t) == Total(s[1..]) + Total(t)`

Therefore: `Total(s + t) == s[0] + Total(s[1..]) + Total(t) == Total(s) + Total(t)`

Let me write the proof bodies:

```json
{
  "Facts.dfy": "module Facts {
  export ViewA provides Total, AppendA
  export ViewB provides Total, AppendB
  function Total(s: seq<int>): int
    decreases |s|
  { if |s| == 0 then 0 else s[0] + Total(s[1..]) }
  lemma {:induction false} AppendA(s: seq<int>, t: seq<int>)
    ensures Total(s + t) == Total(s) + Total(t)
    decreases |s|
  {
    if |s| == 0 {
      assert s + t == t;
    } else {
      assert (s + t)[0] == s[0];
      assert (s + t)[1..] == s[1..] + t;
      AppendA(s[1..], t);
    }
  }
  lemma {:induction false} AppendB(s: seq<int>, t: seq<int>)
    ensures Total(s + t) == Total(s) + Total(t)
    decreases |s|
  {
    if |s| == 0 {
      assert s + t == t;
    } else {
      assert (s + t)[0] == s[0];
      assert (s + t)[1..] == s[1..] + t;
      AppendB(s[1..], t);
    }
  }
}
"
}
```

